	(text "use a precision reference here\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 31.75 22.86 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "04919d43-5024-4e0f-b9d6-92cb40a026cf")
	)
	(text "This is a solid core strategy, using precise reference voltages and a low-noise summing inverter to generate a stable, precise, bidirectional bias potential.\n\nHowever, the user's primary engineering objective—monitoring the minuscule, bidirectional picoampere currents essential for accurate nanopore designs—cannot be achieved with this schematic as drawn. The current summing inverters operate as low-impedance voltage sources; they apply the voltage but do not monitor the resulting current that is demanded by the pore load.\n\nTo implement accurate, bidirectional current monitoring, you must transition this architecture from a voltage source to a transimpedance amplifier (TIA) based current measurement system. The existing DAC and precise offset generation circuitry remain highly valuable, but you must insert a new current measurement stage into the main signal path.\n\nThe following section outlines the ideal engineering path for this modification.\n\n### Core Idea: The Current-Sense TIA Stage\n\nThe proper approach to add pA current monitoring while retaining precise bidirectional voltage drive is to implement a high-side current-sense TIA directly into the `>PORE` signal path. This approach uses the output of your existing precise voltage summer as the common-mode reference potential for a dedicated, ultra-low leakage current measurement amplifier.\n\nHere are the specific ideas and stages required to implement this modification:\n\n1. **Stage 1: Bidirectional Voltage Reference Generation (Modify Existing)**\n* Keep the top channel of your current schematic (`U9`, `U13`, `U10`, and associated resistors/pots) exactly as it is. This circuit is ideal for generating the stable, bidirectional bias voltage (`-2.4\\text{V} to $2.4\\text{V}`) with fine offset adjustment.\n* Crucially, this output node (`U10` pin 6) is **no longer connected directly to the `>PORE` electrode**. It now acts as the low-impedance, precise common-mode reference voltage, `$V_{bias\\_ref}$`, for the new measurement stage.\n\n\n2. **Stage 2: Ultra-Low Noise Current Measurement TIA (New Block)**\n* This is the critical picoamp measurement stage. You **must select an ultra-low input bias current op-amp (e.g., LMP7721, $I_b \\approx 3\\text{fA}$ typical)**. The OPA197 from your original schematic ($I_b = 20\\text{pA}$ typical) is **NOT** suitable for this role, as its own bias current would swamp nanoamp-scale nanopore signals.\n* Connect the precise, buffered `$V_{bias\\_ref}$` (output of Stage 1, U10) to the **non-inverting ($+$) input** of the LMP7721.\n* The `>PORE` electrode terminal is connected to the **inverting ($-$) input**. The feedback loop creates a virtual ground on this terminal, holding the electrode precisely at the `$V_{bias\\_ref}$` potential generated by the DAC circuit.\n* Install a large, precision sense resistor ($R_{sense}$) and a small parallel capacitor ($C_{comp}$) in the feedback path from the output to the ($-$) input. The $R_{sense}$ value determines your gain/range (e.g., $100\\text{M}\\Omega$ resistor to measure $\\pm20\\text{nA}$ and output $\\pm2\\text{V}$ relative to the reference).\n* The output voltage from this stage is given by `$V_{out\\_pA} = V_{bias\\_ref} - I_{pore} \\cdot R_{sense}$`.\n\n\n3. **Stage 3: Common-Mode Voltage Subtraction (New Block)**\n* Because the Stage 2 output is referenced to `$V_{bias\\_ref}$`, which is itself swinging $\\pm2.4\\text{V}$, you cannot read this output directly.\n* You **must add a final precision difference amplifier** (this can be an OPA197 or another high-quality precision op-amp). This stage will subtract `$V_{bias\\_ref}$` from `$V_{out\\_pA}$` to provide a clean, ground-referenced voltage representation of *only* the ionic current: `$V_{final\\_current\\_mon} = (V_{out\\_pA} - V_{bias\\_ref}) = - I_{pore} \\cdot R_{sense}$`.\n\n\n4. **Stage 4: Reference Electrode Ground Handling (Modify Existing)**\n* Since the `>PORE` channel now handles both precise voltage drive and current monitoring, the other electrode (`Vbias` channel) must simply act as a stable ground reference (the \"Trans\" electrode).\n* Remove the entire summing inverter circuit (`U12`, `U11`) from the bottom channel entirely.\n* To prevent noise from noisy digital grounds (`GNDA`) from coupling into your reference, it is best practice to connect this 'Trans' reference electrode through a small current-limiting resistor directly to a dedicated, clean **analog ground point (`GNDA`)**, rather than trying to buffer GNDA with a second voltage summer. This provides the stable, precise reference against which the driven electrode potential is measured and maintained."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
